Alloy: winegstreamer: wait for each stream's samples on its own queue.

The media source ran every command, sample requests included, on one serial work queue, and a request waited there
until GStreamer produced that stream's next buffer. A stream can only get its next buffer once the others have been
read: Wine holds one buffer per stream, and the demuxer's queue holds 2 MB. When a file has more than that between
two buffers of one stream, the wait blocked the requests for the other streams, nothing read them, and playback froze
for good. A 3440x1440, 57 Mbit/s QuickTime file with 16-bit PCM audio in half-second chunks (a game's main menu
background) froze on its first audio request; the game then waited forever for the session to
respond, with the cursor still moving.

Requests are still accepted in order on the source's queue, so they stay ordered with Start, Pause and Stop; the wait
for the sample now runs on a queue of the stream's own. After a wait, a source that has stopped drops the sample, and
one that has shut down leaves the parser alone. The token queue now holds its own reference to each token: the
command it came from releases its reference when it finishes.

